{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,170p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\documentclass{article}\n     2\t% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n     3\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4\t\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n     5\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n     6\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n     7\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n     8\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n     9\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n    10\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n    11\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n    12\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n    13\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n    14\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n    15\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n    16\t}\n    17\t% --- end watermark ---\n    18\t\n    19\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n    20\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n    21\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n    22\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n    23\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n    24\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n    25\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n    26\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n    27\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n    28\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n    29\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n    30\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n    31\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n    32\t}\n    33\t\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n    34\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,longtable,graphicx,subcaption}\n    35\t\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n    36\t\\title{Memorization Without Observed Grokking Across MLP Widths}\n    37\t\\author{}\n    38\t\\newcommand{\\TM}{T_{\\mathrm{M}}}\n    39\t\\newcommand{\\TG}{T_{\\mathrm{G}}}\n    40\t\\newcommand{\\LH}{L_H}\n    41\t\n    42\t\\begin{document}\n    43\t\\maketitle\n    44\t\n    45\t\\begin{abstract}\n    46\tDoes increasing model size change delayed generalization, or only the speed of fitting the training data? We study this distinction in full-batch ReLU multilayer perceptrons on modular addition and subtraction. A fixed learning-rate protocol crosses three hidden widths with two AdamW weight-decay settings and three initialization seeds, producing 36 completed training records. Memorization and generalization are defined by separate sustained accuracy thresholds. Within 20,000 updates, 30 records attain memorization but none attains generalization. Wider networks fit earlier without decay and fit where the narrowest networks fail with positive decay; these differences do not establish faster grokking. An audit of the reported endpoint-substituted lag shows that its positive 500-update width contrast, including a degenerate seed interval, follows from fitting times and censoring rather than observed generalization. The evidence supports a finite-horizon separation between width-dependent fitting and unobserved generalization, while leaving the eventual dependence of grokking time on model size unidentified.\n    47\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    48\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_contrast]]\n    49\t\\end{abstract}\n    50\t\n    51\t\\section{Introduction}\n    52\t\n    53\tSmall algorithmic datasets offer an unusually transparent setting for investigating generalization: the target rule is known, every possible input can be enumerated, and training performance can be separated from performance on unseen inputs. Yet fitting such data need not reveal when a network learns the underlying operation. Grokking names the striking case in which generalization emerges long after the training data have been fitted \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. This separation raises a basic question about scale: does a larger network change the arrival of generalization, or simply change the point from which the delay is measured?\n    54\t\n    55\tThe distinction matters because a delay is a difference between event times. Earlier memorization can increase a measured delay even if generalization is unchanged. Conversely, replacing an unobserved generalization event with the training endpoint can produce an apparently precise contrast without any generalization event having occurred. A study of model size therefore needs to report fitting, generalization, and non-attainment separately, rather than infer one from another. Training accuracy alone is particularly inadequate as a proxy for learning an algorithm; the broader distinction between finite-sample fitting and useful generalization is well established \\citep{Zhang2017Rethinking}.\n    56\t\n    57\tWe examine a controlled width-by-decay grid for a single fixed optimization recipe on addition and subtraction modulo 97. The models are one-hidden-layer ReLU networks with concatenated one-hot inputs. Hidden width varies over 16, 64, and 256; all other architectural choices are fixed. Two decay settings and three initialization seeds give 36 training records. Our working explanation is deliberately limited: width may remove a fitting bottleneck without making generalization observable within the same computational horizon. A stronger claim, that width changes an eventual generalization clock, requires events that this study does not observe.\n    58\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    59\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L656-L660]]\n    60\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L789-L871]]\n    61\t\n    62\tThe results make this separation concrete. All wider networks attain sustained memorization at the first noninitial checkpoint. Narrow networks fit later without decay and do not attain memorization with positive decay. Nevertheless, no condition reaches sustained held-out accuracy of 95\\% within 20,000 updates. Thus, the observed size dependence is a fitting result, not an estimate of the size dependence of grokking time.\n    63\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    64\t\n    65\tOur contributions are an explicit finite-horizon width comparison across related modular operations, a complete seed-level account of fitting and generalization non-attainment, and an algebraic audit of an endpoint-substituted lag statistic. We show why a nonzero width contrast and a zero-width seed interval can coexist with no observed generalization. These findings delimit what the evaluated configuration establishes; they neither rule out later grokking nor choose among proposed neural mechanisms.\n    66\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_ci_low]]\n    67\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_ci_high]]\n    68\t\n    69\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    70\t  \\centering\n    71\t  \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/study_overview.png}\n    72\t  \\caption{Study design and observation boundary. Addition and subtraction modulo 97 are evaluated under a shared fixed-rate MLP protocol crossing three hidden widths, two weight-decay settings, and three initialization seeds. Memorization requires training accuracy of at least 99\\%; generalization requires held-out accuracy of at least 95\\%, each for three consecutive checkpoints. Non-attainment at 20,000 updates is not a measured generalization delay. The diagram summarizes the design, not a proposed neural circuit.}\n    73\t  \\label{fig:overview}\n    74\t  % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    75\t  % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L721-L743]]\n    76\t\\end{figure}\n    77\t\n    78\t\\section{Related Work}\n    79\t\n    80\t\\paragraph{Grokking and candidate explanations.}\n    81\t\\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} establish delayed generalization on small algorithmically generated datasets and describe its dependence on dataset size. Our experiment uses this setting to distinguish finite-budget fitting from generalization-event timing; it is not a replication of a particular delayed-generalization trajectory. \\citet{Liu2023Omnigrok} relate grokking to training and test loss landscapes as a function of weight norm and demonstrate its presence beyond algorithmic data. \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} propose competition between faster memorizing solutions and more norm-efficient generalizing solutions. \\citet{Kumar2024LazyRich} analyze a transition from lazy to feature-learning dynamics, including a two-layer polynomial-regression setting. These explanations motivate keeping optimization, regularization, and representation effects distinct. Our recorded norms and losses do not measure circuit efficiency or a lazy-to-rich transition, and cannot discriminate these accounts.\n    82\t\n    83\t\\paragraph{Capacity and generalization need not move together.}\n    84\t\\citet{Zhang2017Rethinking} demonstrate that fitting training labels and explaining generalization are different problems. \\citet{Nakkiran2020DoubleDescent} show that increasing model size or training duration need not yield monotone test performance. \\citet{Davies2023Unifying} propose a common pattern-learning-speed perspective on grokking and double descent, including variation in capacity. This context cautions against treating a width effect on training accuracy as sufficient evidence about held-out learning. Our grid is too sparse, and its accuracies too uniformly low, to establish a double-descent curve or a general capacity law.\n    85\t\n    86\t\\paragraph{Regularization and the role of the learning rate.}\n    87\tAdamW decouples weight decay from the loss-gradient update \\citep{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. However, its per-update parameter shrinkage still depends on the learning rate used in that update. We therefore compare decay arms under one shared constant rate rather than interpret a change in the learning-rate recipe as a clean intervention on decay alone. The literature discussion is restricted to exact, permitted starter sources; we make no exhaustive priority or novelty claim from this restricted coverage.\n    88\t\n    89\t\\section{Method}\n    90\t\\label{sec:method}\n    91\t\n    92\t\\subsection{Problem, Data, and Evaluation Target}\n    93\t\n    94\tFor a pair $(a,b)\\in\\{0,\\ldots,p-1\\}^2$, the target is either $(a+b)\\bmod p$ or $(a-b)\\bmod p$, with $p=97$. Each operation has $p^2=9,409$ possible ordered pairs. The final evaluation uses a deterministic subset of 4,705 pairs for each operation, with 1,411 training pairs and 3,294 held-out pairs. Thus the training set is approximately 30\\% of the retained subset, not 30\\% of the entire operation table; it covers approximately 15\\% of all possible inputs. Addition and subtraction use independently constructed operation-specific partitions.\n    95\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    96\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L588-L613]]\n    97\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/benchmark.json]]\n    98\t\n    99\tThe partition is reproducible without downloading data. Each input and operation are encoded in canonical JSON, normalized, and hashed. Sorting the hashes separates the development subset from the final subset. A second hash ordering with a fixed training prefix assigns the training portion within the final subset. The split is fixed across initialization seeds and widths. Consequently, seed variability measures training-initialization variability on a fixed split, not uncertainty over fresh data partitions. The held-out set is used for recording performance, not for gradient updates. Appendix~\\ref{app:construction} specifies the construction and readable source locations.\n   100\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L588-L631]]\n   101\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L825-L870]]\n   102\t\n   103\tWe report both operations rather than treating subtraction as an unseen transfer task. Each network is independently trained from scratch on its own operation. The shared architecture and optimization recipe permit a condition-specific replication of the width comparison, but not a claim of cross-task representation transfer.\n   104\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   105\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L656-L660]]\n   106\t\n   107\t\\subsection{Working Explanation and Falsifiable Predictions}\n   108\t\n   109\tThe working explanation separates \\emph{fitting capacity under the chosen optimizer} from \\emph{learning a held-out algorithm}. Increasing width can make the finite training table easier to fit without supplying evidence that the network acquires the arithmetic rule. In a regularized setting, the fitting requirement may become more demanding, so that the narrowest network no longer attains the same training criterion even though wider networks do. This is an explanation of operational fitting behavior, not a claim that parameter count alone determines representational capacity.\n   110\t\n   111\tThe corresponding finite-horizon predictions are explicit. At fixed decay, wider networks should attain the training criterion no later than the narrowest model, or attain it where the narrowest does not. If increased width is sufficient to reveal delayed generalization in this protocol, at least one wider condition should additionally cross the held-out criterion within the budget. The former prediction can be checked using fitting events; the latter can be falsified for this particular horizon. An eventual equality, ordering, or equivalence of generalization times cannot be tested unless those times are observed or otherwise bounded more informatively. These are interpretive predictions for the retained design, not a claim of preregistration or an independent confirmatory test.\n   112\t\n   113\t\\subsection{Width and Decay Comparisons}\n   114\t\n   115\tThe input vector is $x=[e_a;e_b]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{194}$, a concatenation of two one-hot encodings. The classifier is\n   116\t\\begin{equation}\n   117\t f_\\theta(a,b)=W_2\\operatorname{ReLU}(W_1x+b_1)+b_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{97}.\n   118\t\\end{equation}\n   119\tThe hidden width $w\\in\\{16,64,256\\}$ gives $292w+97$ trainable parameters: 4,769, 18,785, and 74,849, respectively. Both linear layers have biases. The implementation uses the default PyTorch linear-layer initialization without a width-specific logit-scale calibration. Therefore, the comparison holds the layer family fixed but does not isolate width from all initialization-scale effects.\n   120\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   121\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L802-L850]]\n   122\t\n   123\tEach condition minimizes full-batch cross-entropy with AdamW. The fixed learning rate is $\\eta=0.01$ throughout training; weight decay is $\\lambda\\in\\{0,1\\}$, with optimizer coefficients $(0.9,0.999)$ and numerical constant $10^{-8}$. All model parameters are passed to the same optimizer without separate bias exclusions. The recipe controller exposes only a bounded open-loop schedule and receives no training metrics. Its evaluated constant-rate setting is identical across operations, widths, decays, and seeds. The frozen controller and evaluator, rather than proposal descriptions, define the implemented method.\n   124\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/controller/src/program.py#L46-L78]]\n   125\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L842-L870]]\n   126\t\n   127\tFor constant $\\eta$, the multiplicative shrinkage contribution of AdamW alone is $(1-\\eta\\lambda)^t$ after $t$ updates. This algebra explains why changing a learning-rate recipe would change both gradient updates and accumulated shrinkage. It does not predict the norm or accuracy of the trained network, whose gradient contributions are not removed. We do not compare the fixed recipe empirically against the original lower-rate configuration because no matched final evaluation of that configuration is retained. Nor do we reinterpret development scores as final-task measurements.\n   128\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/controller/src/EXPERIMENT_SCOPE.md]]\n   129\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L846-L870]]\n   130\t\n   131\t\\subsection{Training Protocol and Sustained Events}\n   132\t\n   133\tThe evaluation crosses two operations, three widths, two decay values, and seeds 1009, 2027, and 3037. Training runs for $H=20,000$ full-batch updates, with an initial measurement and measurements every 500 updates. The worker uses deterministic PyTorch algorithms, one compute thread, and one interoperation thread. Each condition resets the model and optimizer; no weights are transferred between conditions. The environment specifies NumPy 2.1.3 and PyTorch 2.5.1+cpu. Source evidence, dependencies, and output-to-script mappings are included with the supplementary material.\n   134\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   135\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L789-L799]]\n   136\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/requirements.txt]]\n   137\t\n   138\tLet $A_{\\mathrm{tr}}(t)$ and $A_{\\mathrm{ho}}(t)$ denote checkpoint accuracies. We define $\\TM$ as the first checkpoint whose training accuracy and next two checkpoint accuracies all reach 99\\%. Likewise, $\\TG$ uses a held-out threshold of 95\\%. A recorded crossing is timestamped at the start of the sustained sequence, although it is confirmed only after observing the following checkpoints. Events near the endpoint without sufficient confirming measurements do not satisfy the rule. These operational event times are checkpoint-resolved, not continuous measurements of the underlying transition.\n   139\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L721-L743]]\n   140\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   141\t\n   142\tLate-window accuracy averages the final five checkpoints, from 18,000 through 20,000 updates. It is a descriptive endpoint summary and is not interchangeable with either sustained-event criterion. We preserve all completed records, including fitting failures and held-out non-attainment. All 36 records complete, so the results contain neither missing trajectories nor a denominator restricted to fitting-capable models. No new training is performed for this paper; the figures and tables recompute summaries from the sealed final observations.\n   143\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   144\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/completion_fraction]]\n   145\t\n   146\t\\subsection{Capped Lag and Statistical Reporting}\n   147\t\\label{sec:lag}\n   148\t\n   149\tTo audit the retained lag summary, we reproduce its exact definition:\n   150\t\\begin{equation}\n   151\t\\LH=\\begin{cases}\n   152\tH,&\\TM\\text{ unobserved},\\\\\n   153\t\\max\\{0,\\min(\\TG,H)-\\TM\\},&\\TM\\text{ observed},\n   154\t\\end{cases}\n   155\t\\label{eq:lag}\n   156\t\\end{equation}\n   157\twhere an unobserved $\\TG$ is replaced by $H$. This is an administratively capped statistic, not an uncensored grokking time. In particular, assigning $H$ when memorization is unobserved does not define a genuine post-memorization waiting time. When memorization is observed but generalization is not, $H-\\TM$ is the observed waiting period without an attained event.\n   158\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L728-L743]]\n   159\t\n   160\tFor seed $s$ and decay $\\lambda$, the paired extreme-width contrast averages over operations:\n   161\t\\begin{equation}\n   162\tD_s(\\lambda)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{o\\in\\{+, -\\}}\n   163\t\\big[\\LH(o,16,\\lambda,s)-\\LH(o,256,\\lambda,s)\\big].\n   164\t\\label{eq:contrast}\n   165\t\\end{equation}\n   166\tWe reproduce the supplied mean and Student-$t$ interval across the three paired seed contrasts, as well as the difference of mean contrasts between decay arms. All individual seeds are shown in the plots, and descriptive means and sample standard deviations are used where appropriate. The checkpoints, operations, and decay arms are not treated as independent replications. With no generalization events, we do not estimate a distribution of eventual generalization times or infer a population event probability from the pooled 36 conditions.\n   167\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L746-L773]]\n   168\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   169\t\n   170\t\\section{Results}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
