{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"wc -c literature/*.txt; for path in literature/*.txt; do printf '\\\\n--- %s ---\\\\n' \\\"\"'$path\"; rg -n -A3 -B1 '\"'Abstract:|abstract|Abstract' \\\"\"'$path\"; done; cat REPRODUCIBILITY.md'","aggregated_output":" 1392 literature/1611.03530.txt\n 1385 literature/1711.05101.txt\n  904 literature/1912.02292.txt\n 1133 literature/2201.02177.txt\n 1082 literature/2210.01117.txt\n  939 literature/2303.06173.txt\n 1260 literature/2309.02390.txt\n 1957 literature/2310.06110.txt\n10052 total\n\n--- literature/1611.03530.txt ---\n4-\n5:Abstract consulted:\n6-Despite their massive size, successful deep artificial neural networks can exhibit a remarkably small difference between training and test performance. Conventional wisdom attributes small generalization error either to properties of the model family, or to the regularization techniques used during training.\n7-Through extensive systematic experiments, we show how these traditional approaches fail to explain why large neural networks generalize well in practice. Specifically, our experiments establish that state-of-the-art convolutional networks for image classification trained with stochastic gradient methods easily fit a random labeling of the training data. This phenomenon is qualitatively unaffected by explicit regularization, and occurs even if we replace the true images by completely unstructured random noise. We corroborate these experimental findings with a theoretical construction showing that simple depth two neural networks already have perfect finite sample expressivity as soon as the number of parameters exceeds the number of data points as it usually does in practice.\n8-We interpret our experimental findings by comparison with traditional models.\n\n--- literature/1711.05101.txt ---\n4-\n5:Abstract consulted:\n6-L$_2$ regularization and weight decay regularization are equivalent for standard stochastic gradient descent (when rescaled by the learning rate), but as we demonstrate this is \\emph{not} the case for adaptive gradient algorithms, such as Adam. While common implementations of these algorithms employ L$_2$ regularization (often calling it \"weight decay\" in what may be misleading due to the inequivalence we expose), we propose a simple modification to recover the original formulation of weight decay regularization by \\emph{decoupling} the weight decay from the optimization steps taken w.r.t. the loss function. We provide empirical evidence that our proposed modification (i) decouples the optimal choice of weight decay factor from the setting of the learning rate for both standard SGD and Adam and (ii) substantially improves Adam's generalization performance, allowing it to compete with SGD with momentum on image classification datasets (on which it was previously typically outperformed by the latter). Our proposed decoupled weight decay has already been adopted by many researchers, and the community has implemented it in TensorFlow and PyTorch; the complete source code for our experiments is available at this https URL\n\n--- literature/1912.02292.txt ---\n4-\n5:Abstract consulted:\n6-We show that a variety of modern deep learning tasks exhibit a \"double-descent\" phenomenon where, as we increase model size, performance first gets worse and then gets better. Moreover, we show that double descent occurs not just as a function of model size, but also as a function of the number of training epochs. We unify the above phenomena by defining a new complexity measure we call the effective model complexity and conjecture a generalized double descent with respect to this measure. Furthermore, our notion of model complexity allows us to identify certain regimes where increasing (even quadrupling) the number of train samples actually hurts test performance.\n\n--- literature/2201.02177.txt ---\n4-\n5:Abstract consulted:\n6-In this paper we propose to study generalization of neural networks on small algorithmically generated datasets. In this setting, questions about data efficiency, memorization, generalization, and speed of learning can be studied in great detail. In some situations we show that neural networks learn through a process of \"grokking\" a pattern in the data, improving generalization performance from random chance level to perfect generalization, and that this improvement in generalization can happen well past the point of overfitting. We also study generalization as a function of dataset size and find that smaller datasets require increasing amounts of optimization for generalization. We argue that these datasets provide a fertile ground for studying a poorly understood aspect of deep learning: generalization of overparametrized neural networks beyond memorization of the finite training dataset.\n\n--- literature/2210.01117.txt ---\n4-\n5:Abstract consulted:\n6-Grokking, the unusual phenomenon for algorithmic datasets where generalization happens long after overfitting the training data, has remained elusive. We aim to understand grokking by analyzing the loss landscapes of neural networks, identifying the mismatch between training and test losses as the cause for grokking. We refer to this as the \"LU mechanism\" because training and test losses (against model weight norm) typically resemble \"L\" and \"U\", respectively. This simple mechanism can nicely explain many aspects of grokking: data size dependence, weight decay dependence, the emergence of representations, etc. Guided by the intuitive picture, we are able to induce grokking on tasks involving images, language and molecules. In the reverse direction, we are able to eliminate grokking for algorithmic datasets. We attribute the dramatic nature of grokking for algorithmic datasets to representation learning.\n\n--- literature/2303.06173.txt ---\n4-\n5:Abstract consulted:\n6-A principled understanding of generalization in deep learning may require unifying disparate observations under a single conceptual framework. Previous work has studied \\emph{grokking}, a training dynamic in which a sustained period of near-perfect training performance and near-chance test performance is eventually followed by generalization, as well as the superficially similar \\emph{double descent}. These topics have so far been studied in isolation. We hypothesize that grokking and double descent can be understood as instances of the same learning dynamics within a framework of pattern learning speeds. We propose that this framework also applies when varying model capacity instead of optimization steps, and provide the first demonstration of model-wise grokking.\n\n--- literature/2309.02390.txt ---\n4-\n5:Abstract consulted:\n6-One of the most surprising puzzles in neural network generalisation is grokking: a network with perfect training accuracy but poor generalisation will, upon further training, transition to perfect generalisation. We propose that grokking occurs when the task admits a generalising solution and a memorising solution, where the generalising solution is slower to learn but more efficient, producing larger logits with the same parameter norm. We hypothesise that memorising circuits become more inefficient with larger training datasets while generalising circuits do not, suggesting there is a critical dataset size at which memorisation and generalisation are equally efficient. We make and confirm four novel predictions about grokking, providing significant evidence in favour of our explanation. Most strikingly, we demonstrate two novel and surprising behaviours: ungrokking, in which a network regresses from perfect to low test accuracy, and semi-grokking, in which a network shows delayed generalisation to partial rather than perfect test accuracy.\n\n--- literature/2310.06110.txt ---\n4-\n5:Abstract consulted:\n6-We propose that the grokking phenomenon, where the train loss of a neural network decreases much earlier than its test loss, can arise due to a neural network transitioning from lazy training dynamics to a rich, feature learning regime. To illustrate this mechanism, we study the simple setting of vanilla gradient descent on a polynomial regression problem with a two layer neural network which exhibits grokking without regularization in a way that cannot be explained by existing theories. We identify sufficient statistics for the test loss of such a network, and tracking these over training reveals that grokking arises in this setting when the network first attempts to fit a kernel regression solution with its initial features, followed by late-time feature learning where a generalizing solution is identified after train loss is already low. We find that the key determinants of grokking are the rate of feature learning -- which can be controlled precisely by parameters that scale the network output -- and the alignment of the initial features with the target function $y(x)$. We argue this delayed generalization arises when (1) the top eigenvectors of the initial neural tangent kernel and the task labels $y(x)$ are misaligned, but (2) the dataset size is large enough so that it is possible for the network to generalize eventually, but not so large that train loss perfectly tracks test loss at all epochs, and (3) the network begins training in the lazy regime so does not learn features immediately. We conclude with evidence that this transition from lazy (linear model) to rich training (feature learning) can control grokking in more general settings, like on MNIST, one-layer Transformers, and student-teacher networks.\n# Reproduction and evidence boundaries\n\n## Scope\n\nThis bundle analyzes the retained final evaluation. It does not rerun training or\nevaluate a new controller. The central result is finite-horizon memorization\nwithout observed sustained generalization, not a generalization-time or mechanism\nclaim. `paper_data.json` is the unchanged host evidence registry.\n\nThe final qualification sources independently report completed/qualified/true.\nAcceptance does not imply generalization, superiority, or comprehensive validity\ncertification. The diagnostics trust flag remains unverified. The original\nlower-rate configuration has no matched final evaluation here.\n\n## Neural training\n\n- Dependencies: `source_evidence/requirements.txt` pins NumPy and CPU PyTorch.\n- Training implementation: `source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:789` owns feature\n  construction, initialization, AdamW, full-batch cross-entropy, and checkpoints.\n- Controller: `source_evidence/controller/program_spec.toml` points to\n  `source_evidence/controller/src/program.py`. It returns a shared constant rate.\n- Seeds: read the three seed entries in\n  `paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes`.\n- Data: `source_evidence/resources/final/benchmark.json` is retained. The exact\n  generation and partition code is at `source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:588`.\n  Data are synthetic and require no downloads, accounts, or third-party licenses.\n- Measurements: `source_evidence/evaluation_evidence.json` and the corresponding\n  embedded final records in `paper_data.json` are the retained observations.\n\nThe original evaluator accepts `--program_path` and `--results_dir`, but depends\non an external RPC broker. A complete standalone broker launcher is not supplied.\nThese flags alone are not a working independent training reproduction command.\nThe source snapshots make the neural computation inspectable; replaying the full\nexecution context requires the original orchestration infrastructure. No new\ntraining command or unmeasured runtime is invented to conceal this limitation.\n\nThe recorded seed-evaluation durations are available in `analysis/derived_results.json`.\nThey are actual durations, not portable hardware/runtime estimates.\n\n## Recompute tables and quantitative figures\n\nCreate analysis dependencies outside the manuscript tree:\n\n```sh\npython -m venv /tmp/paper-analysis\n/tmp/paper-analysis/bin/pip install -r analysis/plotting_requirements.txt\n/tmp/paper-analysis/bin/python build_artifacts.py\n```\n\nThis reads final records, checks the event and lag computations against stored\nvalues, and writes the following artifacts without training:\n\n| Paper artifact | Output | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main event table | `tables/events.tex` | final observations, memorization/generalization fields |\n| Complete seed table | `tables/full_observations.tex` | final traces and event fields |\n| Endpoint diagnostics table | `tables/diagnostics.tex` | final checkpoint losses and parameter norms |\n| Width/decay outcome figure | `figures/width_decay_outcomes.pdf`, `.png` | final five trace checkpoints |\n| Censoring audit figure | `figures/censoring_audit.pdf`, `.png` | observed events and capped lags |\n| Machine-readable derived results | `analysis/derived_results.json` | same final observations |\n\nAll are produced by `build_artifacts.py`. Quantitative PDF exports are vector\ngraphics; PNGs are generated directly at 300 DPI on an 8.25-inch-wide canvas.\nThe concept overview is orchestrator-owned and initially contains a deliberately\nlabeled placeholder. The build script never overwrites a completed concept\nfigure or a figure with a nonempty rendering log. No artificial trajectories\nor synthetic measurements are used.\n\n## Compile and check\n\n```sh\npython .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py --tex paper.tex --bib references.bib\npython .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib --claims claims_grounding.json --paper-dir .\nbash .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir .\nbash .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\n\nCompilation requires LaTeX, BibTeX and Poppler `pdftotext`. The official ICLR 2026\nstyle and bibliography style were retrieved from the exact official template\ndirectory; URLs and file hashes are retained in\n`source_evidence/template_provenance.json`. Review mode remains anonymous.\n\nThe bundled figure validator imports parent-only orchestration code. Where that\nmodule is unavailable locally, schema/density checks are recorded separately and\nthe parent must run its authoritative figure and page-occupancy validator.\n\n## Literature safety and unmet reference quota\n\nOnly the eight exact source identities approved by the strategy were retrieved\nand consulted, at title/author/abstract scope. `literature/retrieve_approved.py`\ncontains that allowlist; `references_provenance.json` identifies the consulted\nfiles and their provenance. No citation graphs, recommendations, or broad\nwidth/grokking searches were consulted. Off-target metadata in the initial\nretrieval was not used as substantive evidence. No prohibited identity was\ninferred or intentionally accessed.\n\nThe requested minimum of 25 references cannot be met under the strategy's\neight-identity safety boundary. The manuscript cites eight real consulted\nsources rather than padding the bibliography with unrelated or unconsulted\nentries. This is an explicit unresolved submission requirement, not a successful\nreference-count validation. See `submission_readiness.json` for final status.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
